  <article>
    <nav class="breadcrumb"><a href="/learn">Learn</a> / Portfolio vs. résumé</nav>
    <span class="mono-label">GUIDE</span>
    <h1>Is a portfolio better than a résumé for breaking into tech in 2026?</h1>

    <p class="answer-capsule">In most cases, yes. A résumé is a list of claims an employer has to take on faith, while a portfolio lets them see your work and judge it directly. With skills-based hiring now standard, that shift toward evidence matters more than ever. The catch is that a portfolio only wins when the work is good, relevant to the role, and seen by the right people.</p>

    <div class="tldr">
      <ul>
        <li>A résumé asserts; a portfolio demonstrates. Employers increasingly want to judge the work, not trust a summary of it.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers used skills-based hiring in 2025</a>, and résumé reliance is declining (TestGorilla).</li>
        <li>A portfolio only helps if the work is strong, relevant, and evaluated. Volume of unrelated projects does not move the needle.</li>
        <li>It is not portfolio versus résumé. Use the résumé to pass screening, and the portfolio to win the decision.</li>
        <li>The portfolio piece that works hardest is one scored against a professional bar, so its quality is established, not assumed.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Why is the résumé losing ground?</h2>
      <p>A résumé was always a compromise. An employer cannot watch you work before they hire you, so for decades the résumé stood in as a compressed summary of claims they had to mostly believe. That worked when degrees and titles were reliable proxies for ability. It works less well now, because the proxies have weakened and because the tools to evaluate skill directly finally exist. TestGorilla's survey found skills-based hiring reached <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">85% of employers in 2025</a> while résumé use fell, which is the market saying out loud that it would rather see the work than read about it.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What makes a portfolio actually work?</h2>
      <p>This is where most advice oversells the portfolio, so be precise. A portfolio is only better than a résumé if it does something a résumé cannot: prove quality. A folder of side projects nobody evaluated proves you were busy, not that you are good. The version that changes a hiring decision has three properties. The work is real, the kind the job actually involves. It is relevant, matched to the specific role rather than a scattershot of everything you have ever made. And ideally it has been evaluated against a professional standard, so a recruiter is not left guessing whether it is any good. Strip away any of those and a clean, honest résumé can outperform a weak portfolio.</p>
      <p>For a student in particular, the evaluation is the missing piece. You can build a real, relevant project on your own, but you usually cannot tell a recruiter whether it meets a professional bar, and neither can they, quickly. A score from someone qualified to give it closes that gap and turns your work from something you made into something that has been vetted.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>So should you build a portfolio or a résumé?</h2>
      <p>Both, with different jobs. Treat the résumé as the key that gets you through the first door. Many application systems and recruiters still expect one, and automated screening often reads it before a human does, so a clean, keyword-honest résumé is table stakes. Then treat the portfolio as the thing that actually wins the room once you are in it. The résumé buys you the few seconds of attention; the evaluated work earns the decision. Framing it as one versus the other is the mistake. They sit at different stages of the same funnel.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How do you build a portfolio that gets you hired?</h2>
      <p>Start from the role, not from your hobbies. Pick the job you want, do two or three real tasks from it, and get each one evaluated so you know it holds up. Show your reasoning, because in tech roles how you think is often what is being judged. Then put the work where decision-makers can find it rather than burying it in a personal site nobody visits. zero is built to produce exactly this kind of portfolio: you take on company-shaped tasks, your work is scored against the bar professionals are held to, and the scored result becomes a receipt recruiters can evaluate, free for students because recruiters pay for the access. A portfolio of evaluated, recruiter-visible work is the strongest version of the thing this whole question is reaching for.</p>
    </section>

